Microsoft used its Ignite event on 17 November 2026 to announce what it calls the AI Transparency Initiative. The pitch is that as AI systems act more on their own, businesses need a way to see what those systems did and to check it independently. This post explains what the announcement actually says, what it leaves out, and what a small business can do today without waiting for any standard.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Microsoft builds its own AI models and products, so it is an interested party in this topic. Stackbrook did not test any Microsoft product, and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not financial or legal advice.*

## The short version

![Diagram showing the three parts of the See, Verify, Control framework from Microsoft's AI Transparency Initiative announcement](/images/site1/microsoft-ai-transparency-initiative-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages. It restates the framework as Microsoft describes it; it is not an official Microsoft graphic.*

Microsoft's announcement is about direction, not delivery. It names a principle, a three-part framework and a group of partners. It does not give a timeline, a list of finished standards or any pricing. If you were hoping for a new setting to turn on this week, the page does not describe one.

## What Microsoft says it is doing

According to the announcement, the initiative rests on a simple idea, quoted from the page: "Greater AI autonomy requires greater transparency." Microsoft's stated problem is that organizations can see a lot of AI activity but often cannot reconstruct what happened between a request and an outcome, or independently verify how a model behaved.

The company describes three parts:

1. **Standards work.** Microsoft says it will expand its participation in the NIST AI Agent Standards Initiative, and use that research to help develop open, industry-wide standards for AI transparency, security and governance.
2. **Partner collaboration.** It says it is working with more than 40 organizations on open standards meant to make AI systems accountable, checkable by outsiders and safe by design, across models and providers.
3. **Product integration.** Microsoft says it will build the results into Microsoft AI, Microsoft Foundry and Microsoft Copilot, so customers can use the resulting evidence with security and compliance tools, from Microsoft or from other providers.

Only five partners are named in the article we read: Accenture, Adobe, KPMG, Mastercard and Volvo Cars. The rest of the 40-plus are not listed there. The announcement says the work is meant to cover both closed and open-weight models, so a company could change models without weakening its security and compliance oversight.

## See, Verify, Control

The framework is the most concrete part of the announcement, and it is easy to restate in plain terms.

**See** means being able to tell what the AI was asked and authorized to do, which model and runtime ran, which data and tools it touched and what it actually did, while watching for risk signals on an ongoing basis.

**Verify** means that evidence should identify its source: the model itself, the software around it, or an independent system. The point, as Microsoft puts it, is to let an organization check what a model reports about itself against records collected from outside.

**Control** means enforcing policy before consequential actions happen and keeping authority over what data agents can access, so oversight does not depend on which model or provider is behind the agent.

In our view none of these ideas is exotic. They are close to ordinary good practice for any system that can act on your behalf: log what it does, do not rely only on its own account, and set limits in advance. What is new is the attempt to make them common across vendors.

## What is the NIST connection?

NIST, the US standards agency, announced its own AI Agent Standards Initiative on 17 February 2026. Its Center for AI Standards and Innovation leads it, with NIST's Information Technology Laboratory and the National Science Foundation among the collaborators. NIST lists three pillars: industry-led agent standards, community-led open source agent protocols, and research on agent security and identity. Its page named a request for information on agent security and a concept paper on agent identity and authorization, with comment deadlines in March and April 2026, and said further deliverables would follow without giving dates. We did not look for what NIST has published since, so check NIST directly for its current status.

## What the announcement does not say

Reading the Microsoft page closely, several things are simply absent:

- **No timelines that we found.** There are no milestones or delivery dates for standards or product features.
- **No named standards.** It does not say which standards will be produced, or in what format.
- **No full partner list.** Most participants are not identified.
- **No evidence format.** There is no schema or disclosure template for the "evidence" it talks about.
- **No availability or pricing** for Microsoft AI, Foundry or Copilot features tied to this work.
- **No account of how the NIST collaboration will work,** or what Microsoft's expanded role means in practice.

The page also says accountability stays with the organization using the AI, and that blaming the AI is not an answer. Take that as Microsoft's position; it is not legal guidance.

## What a small business can do now

The following is Stackbrook's own reading, not something Microsoft or NIST recommends in the announcement. You do not need to wait for standards to apply the See, Verify, Control idea at small scale.

- **See:** keep a short list of every AI tool your team uses, what data each one can reach, and who owns the account. Turn on any activity or audit history the tool offers.
- **Verify:** for anything an AI tool does that touches money, customers or published content, keep a record you can check outside the tool, such as an email confirmation or an invoice, rather than relying on the tool's own summary.
- **Control:** limit what each tool can access to the minimum it needs, require a person to approve consequential actions, and set spending or sending limits where the tool allows them.

If you are choosing between tools, you can reasonably ask a vendor what logs it keeps, how long, and whether you can export them. Our earlier guide on [what to check before giving an AI tool your customer data](/posts/ai-tool-customer-data-checklist) covers related questions.

## The takeaway

The AI Transparency Initiative is a statement of intent from a large vendor, backed by a handful of named partners and a promise of open standards. Microsoft says it will expand its participation in NIST's agent work, and in our view the framework is sensible, but as of 18 November 2026 the announcement offers no dates, no finished standards and no product details. Watch for the partner list, the first published standard and any product documentation. Until then, the useful part is the habit: know what your AI tools can do, keep records you can check independently, and set limits before something goes wrong.
